/groups.ts
Purpose: groups.dat REST API (Phase 8) — GET (overlay or stock fallback, +baseHash) , POST preview, gated PUT (optional baseHash → conditional, 409 carries currentHash) with copyover note, DELETE (revert to compiled table); same never-crash safe() contract as areas/skills.

/import.test.ts
Purpose: .are import tests both gate states: clean preview (no writes) + commit (byte-identical disk, area.lst registration, copyover flag), broken/overlap 400s with zero writes, overwrite 409-then-backup, >1mb body via the scoped parser, oversized/binary/traversal store guards.

/import.ts
Purpose: .are import API (Phase 10) — POST /api/import/area/preview (quarantine report, never writes) + gated PUT /api/import/area commit; scoped 2mb JSON parser registered before the app-wide 1mb one.

/map.test.ts
Purpose: /api/map tests: single-area shape with internal/external/dangling exits told apart (external carries file+name), 404 unlisted / 400 invalid name, world aggregation of areas + directional links with a broken file tolerated.

/map.ts
Purpose: Read-only map aggregates (Phase 12) — GET /api/map/:file (rooms+exits with locks, cross-area targets resolved via worldVnumIndex to {file,name}, Phase 12b `warps` from R-script `warp <vnum>` lines) and GET /api/map (world graph: area nodes + directional cross-area exit links with per-exit detail); never writes, never audited, unparseable files listed with parseError.

/presence.test.ts
Purpose: Presence tests: registry unit (heartbeat/list/refresh, lazy TTL expiry via injected clock), standalone routes (anonymous heartbeat, 400 guards), full-app auth-on (401 anon, credential-named entry, open GET, never audited). Phase 4 case: a centrally-issued token heartbeats under its auth-server label via the same fake-introspect pattern as auth.test.ts.

/presence.ts
Purpose: Advisory presence routes (Phase 11): guarded POST /api/presence heartbeat (name from the acting credential, returned to the caller) + open GET listing live entries; scoped 4kb parser; excluded from auditing.

/rebuild.test.ts
Purpose: Phase 15/G route tests via a minimal standalone app (registerRebuildRoutes only, injected RoleStore + RebuildStore with a mock CommandRunner — never a real docker call): 401/403 guard matrix, 501 when MUD_REBUILD_ENABLED is off (even for master), 202 + pipeline actually runs in the background for master, 409 while already running, an admin-tier account's USERNAME (not label/accountId) becomes the persisted pipeline actor, a no-grant account actor 403s (Phase G), GET /api/rebuild/status works for any recognized actor and reports the current persisted status.

/rebuild.ts
Purpose: Phase 15 (Step 7) — POST /api/rebuild (requireRebuildAllowed-gated, no body, checks rebuildEnabled AFTER the guard so an unauthenticated caller never learns whether it's on, 409 if already running, `void rebuildStore.runPipeline(actor)` then 202 immediately — same idiom as reload.ts) + GET /api/rebuild/status (requireAnyActor-gated — operationally sensitive, not open-GET content). describeActor() picks the account's USERNAME as the pipeline's actor label when available (falls back to accountId), distinct from audit.ts's describeActor() which shows the key's label. Phase G: registerRebuildRoutes takes a RoleStore param (threaded to both checkRebuildEligibility call sites) — the app.ts caller constructs it once and shares it with registerRoleRoutes.

/reload.ts
Purpose: POST /api/reload — writes the hot (reload.signal, per-file) or copyover (copyover.signal) sentinel the game polls; write-gated.

/roles.test.ts
Purpose: Phase G route tests: GET /api/roles/me shape per actor kind; GET /api/roles 403s a plain account (no management standing) but 200s master/hub-admin/hub-owner; POST /api/roles/:accountId — 400 unknown tier, 400 'owner' (even for master), master grants admin, a hub-global admin/owner with NO local grant of their own can bootstrap another account to admin (the Decision 4 path), a hub-global moderator (below admin) can grant nothing, a local admin can grant manager/trusted/user but not admin/owner (strictly-below via canManage), a local user (default) can grant nothing, a local API key 403s outright (no accountId).

/roles.ts
Purpose: Phase G role-management surface (Decision 4) — GET /api/roles/me (any actor, own standing only: local tier + hub globalRole + own accountId/username), GET /api/roles (grant list, gated the same as granting), POST /api/roles (set a grant, gated). `canGrant()` is the ceiling rule: 'owner' refused outright regardless of caller; master and a hub-global owner/admin get a flat "up to admin" ceiling (this IS the bootstrap behavior); everyone else needs canManage() strictly-below their OWN local tier. Mirrors auth-server's admin-guard.ts "owner never assignable over HTTP" invariant for the GLOBAL ladder, applied to the local SERVICE_TIERS ladder. 2026-08-16, two live bugs same day, in order: (1) GET /api/roles/me gained `accountId` after a user had no way to discover their own real accountId and typed their USERNAME into the grant form's "Account ID" field instead, silently granting a role to nothing; (2) superseded hours later by moving the grant route itself from `POST /api/roles/:accountId` to `POST /api/roles` with a USERNAME in the body — resolved server-side via auth-server's `resolve-username` service call (same authServerUrl/servicePrivateKeyPath config as the introspect fallback) before ever touching the role store, so an operator never handles a raw accountId at all. `/api/roles/me` now ALSO returns `username` for the same "use it" self-fill button, which fills the username field instead. Unresolvable username = 404; resolution unconfigured = 501, not a crash.

/skills.test.ts
